course: dictionary
lesson: 6981
title: Oh yes it is! Understanding panto
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: A panto is a funny show that families often see at Christmas. Panto is a short, informal way of saying pantomime. Read the words around it to work out its meaning: «Pantomime, or panto, is a British Christmas show where the audience is meant to shout, boo the villain and cheer».
explain: The clues are Christmas, shout, boo and cheer. A show where the audience is allowed to shout and boo is lively and noisy, and that is what a panto is.
explain: Panto can also mean something silly and over-the-top, as in «a bit of a panto». To check, swap in your meaning: "That was a bit of a silly, over-the-top fuss" still makes sense, so you have understood it.
fact: Panto is short for pantomime, and it is informal, so people use it in everyday chat.
fact: A panto is usually based on a fairy tale, such as Cinderella or Aladdin.
fact: A panto has a «dame», a man dressed as a woman, and a villain who gets booed.
fact: The audience shouts «He's behind you!» and «Oh no it isn't!» back at the actors, who reply «Oh yes it is!».
fact: Outside the UK and Ireland, pantomime often means mime, which is acting without words.
exercise: «Theatre happens anywhere, and some shows want silence while a panto wants you to shout "He's behind you!".» What does a panto want from its audience? | To leave early | *To shout | To sleep | Silence
exercise: Ellie says, «That goal was a bit of a panto!» What does she mean? | A long walk in the rain | *Something silly and over-the-top | A quiet, serious moment | A type of cake
exercise: Which fairy tale could a panto be based on? | The Great Fire of London | A weather report | A football match | *Aladdin
test: In a panto, what does the audience usually do to the villain? | *Boo | Cheer | Sleep | Hug
test: In a panto, what is a «dame»? | *A man dressed as a woman | A type of cake | A talking animal | A queen in a castle
exam: Why is «panto» called an informal word? | *It is a short, everyday way of saying pantomime | It is the name of a famous London theatre | It means a silent play with no words | It is a Scottish word for a grey, wet day
